Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `_thread.Abort()` | Timeout enforcement fallback | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `while (true)` in `RunLoop` | Cancellable work loop | No way to exit cooperatively |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call | Not cancellation-aware |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _thread = new Thread(() => RunLoop(_cts.Token));
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        // WaitAny wakes on either a new event OR cancellation
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles); // replaces _newEvent.WaitOne()

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();           // signals token.WaitHandle, wakes WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);      // wait up to 5s for cooperative exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback needed — the loop checks the token
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationToken`** — `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` wakes the blocked thread cooperatively instead of throwing `ThreadInterruptedException`.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → removed** — The loop checks `token.IsCancellationRequested` and exits cleanly. No forcible termination needed.

3. **`while (true)` → `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — The loop cooperates with cancellation.

4. **Added `IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEvent` are `IDisposable`; leaking them causes timer/callback leaks.

5. **Guard on `Dequeue`** — The `WaitAny` can wake from *either* handle, so we must check `_queue.Count > 0` before dequeuing to avoid `InvalidOperationException`.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Replacing `Interrupt` with another `Interrupt` | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046`). Use `CancellationToken` signaling. |
| Assuming `Cancel()` kills the thread instantly | It only sets a flag. The thread exits at the next `WaitAny`/`IsCancellationRequested` check. |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal timers and registered callbacks. |
| Calling `Stop()` then reusing the instance | `CancellationTokenSource` cannot be reset. `Start()` creates a new one — this is already handled above. |
| Forgetting the `_queue.Count` guard | `WaitAny` returns for *either* handle; dequeuing without a check throws on an empty queue. |
